      "writtenOn": "2015-05-29T05:58:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I am not positive on 255 characters limitation.\n\nmysql has this claim about it\u0027s index size limitation, \u0027By default, an index key for a single-column index can be up to 767 bytes\u0027.\n\nSo, is it appropriate to thrown such a warning when mysql is in use? not object to this change but we may consider how many characters is really safe.",
      "revId": "f596bbc5a83ef3653ecbbecb2f93ef4d59cf5fc9",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7a016987_fa659544",
        "filename": "keystone/token/providers/fernet/token_formatters.py",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 155,
      "author": {
        "id": 9142
      },
      "writtenOn": "2015-05-29T10:35:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We are not limiting token size; but just warning in the case it is longer than 255 chars.\n\nWhy 255 ? 255 is commonly the max that fits in the index field of a database table.\n\nIt can be longer in some cases, but in general they are limited to that value. As example, see:\n\n\"The length of a CHAR column is fixed to the length that you declare when you create the table. The length can be any value from 0 to 255.\"\n\nhttps://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/5.7/en/char.html",
      "parentUuid": "7a016987_ae6b66b8",
      "revId": "f596bbc5a83ef3653ecbbecb2f93ef4d59cf5fc9",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
